1) 从告警标签取 target 并执行 curl -sI \u003ctarg:
1) Take target from the alert labels and confirm the actual status code with curl -sI <target>;
2) Among 4xx, 403 is the most common: check whether hotlink protection, the Referer allowlist, or URL authentication was just changed; a 404 means the origin resource was deleted;
3) A 5xx means the origin fetch failed, so curl the origin address directly to check its health and confirm the origin Host header matches the origin site configuration;
4) If it is a CDN-side configuration problem, roll back the most recent change and purge the cache.

1) 从告警标签取 target，执行 curl -w '%{time_name:
1) Take target from the alert labels and break down the timing with curl -w '%{time_namelookup} %{time_connect} %{time_starttransfer}\n' -o /dev/null -s <target>;
2) A slow first byte with a fast connect means the origin fetch is slow; check origin response time and the CDN cache hit ratio;
3) For a low hit ratio, review the cache rules: content may be marked Cache-Control: no-cache or carry random query parameters that prevent caching;
4) If the origin itself is slow, optimise it first and consider enabling origin keep-alive and prefetching on the CDN.

1) 从告警标签取 target，用 curl -w 拆解 DNS/TCP/TL:
1) Take target from the alert labels and use curl -w to break the time into DNS, TCP, TLS, first byte, and transfer, then find the dominant stage;
2) For a slow DNS stage, check the resolution chain and TTL settings; for a slow TLS stage, confirm the certificate chain is not overly long and session resumption is enabled;
3) For a slow transfer stage, it is usually an oversized file or saturated edge bandwidth; evaluate compression and chunking;
4) If no single stage dominates, compare probe points across regions to determine whether it is a region-specific node problem and report it to the vendor.

1) 从告警标签取被探测的 target，在本地执行 curl -sv \u00:
1) Take the probed target from the alert labels, reproduce it locally with curl -sv <target>, and use the cdn_probe_result value to identify the failing stage;
2) For value 3 (DNS failure), run dig <domain> to confirm the CNAME still points at the CDN vendor and check whether someone changed the record;
3) For values 1/2, run mtr <CDN node IP> from the probe point to separate a network problem from a CDN node failure, and check the vendor console for node incident notices;
4) For values 5/6, the origin content or status code changed; check first whether the origin was released or has failed.
